# 5. Actual input CSV format

The supplied sample file is:

```text
robbert_kwartierstaat.csv
```

The importer must support this format.

The file is **not a conventional single-table CSV**.

It consists of multiple CSV tables separated by blank rows.

The supplied sample contains four sections:

```text
Place
Person
Marriage
Family
```

The sample currently contains:

* 105 place records;
* 456 person records;
* 229 family/union records in the `Marriage` section;
* 230 family-child links;
* 228 unique family IDs represented in the child-link section.

The real CSV must never be committed to the repository.

---

# 6. Multi-section CSV parser

The importer must not attempt to read the entire file as one rectangular table with one header.

The sections have different numbers of columns.

For example:

```text
Place,...
...
<blank row>

Person,...
...
<blank row>

Marriage,...
...
<blank row>

Family,...
...
```

A normal single-header CSV parser would therefore interpret later sections as malformed rows.

## Required parsing strategy

1. Parse the complete file using a standards-compliant CSV parser into raw rows.
2. Preserve empty rows during initial parsing.
3. Divide parsed rows into sections at completely empty rows.
4. Treat the first row of each section as that section's header.
5. Convert following rows into records using that section's header.
6. Identify the section from its first header value.
7. Pass each recognized section to the appropriate import adapter.

Do not split the raw text using simple string operations before CSV parsing if doing so would break quoted CSV values containing line breaks.

Unknown future sections should generate an informational import message rather than automatically causing the entire import to fail.

---

# 7. Place section

Exact observed header:

```text
Place,
Title,
Name,
Type,
Latitude,
Longitude,
Code,
Enclosed_by,
Date
```

Example conceptual record:

```text
[P0006],,Utrecht,Unknown,52.08...,5.12...,,,
```

## Required field mapping

```ts
Place {
    id: string
    name: string

    latitude?: number
    longitude?: number

    title?: string
    type?: string
    code?: string
    enclosedById?: string
    date?: GenealogyDate

    raw: Record<string, string>
}
```

Square brackets are part of the imported identifier format.

The application may internally normalize:

```text
[P0006]
```

to:

```text
P0006
```

but the transformation must be consistent across every reference.

Preferably preserve both the canonical ID and original imported value.

