# 39. Export appearance controls

Provide fine-grained settings independent from the interactive screen.

At minimum support:

* number of generations;
* page dimensions;
* font size;
* node dimensions;
* generation spacing;
* horizontal spacing;
* line thickness;
* name display;
* birth-date display;
* death-date display;
* place display;
* title;
* legend;
* colour/monochrome;
* background;
* crop to content.

Export configuration must not require changing the normal interactive visualization.

---

# 40. Raster resolution

PNG export must support explicit DPI/resolution settings.

At minimum:

```text
150 DPI
300 DPI
600 DPI
Custom
```

The calculated pixel dimensions should be shown before export.

For example:

```text
A3 at 300 DPI
→ calculated pixel dimensions
```

Warn users when requested raster dimensions are unusually large and may exhaust browser memory.

SVG and vector PDF should be described as resolution-independent where appropriate.

---

# 41. Local persistence

The basic application must work without saving imported data.

Optional local persistence may use:

```text
IndexedDB
```

If implemented:

* it must stay on the device;
* it must be optional;
* users must be able to remove saved datasets;
* the interface must make clear that storage is local.

Do not use remote persistence.

---

# 42. Dataset replacement

Users must be able to:

* load a CSV;
* replace the current CSV;
* reload an updated export;
* clear the dataset.

Loading a new dataset must reset all state derived from the old dataset.

Do not automatically merge datasets.

---

# 43. Performance

The application should comfortably support several thousand people.

Avoid repeated full-array scans during visualization.

Use indexed lookups.

Genealogy traversal must:

* detect cycles;
* avoid infinite recursion;
* memoize expensive analyses where helpful;
* handle deep ancestry without blocking the browser unnecessarily.

The supplied 456-person dataset should be considered a small normal dataset rather than a stress test.

