# 60. Acceptance criteria

The first production version is acceptable when all of the following succeed:

1. Open the application from GitHub Pages.
2. Confirm the application loads correctly below the repository path.
3. Select a genealogy CSV from the local computer.
4. Confirm no genealogy file upload occurs.
5. Parse the four CSV sections correctly.
6. Import people, places, families, and child relationships.
7. Resolve parent relationships through family IDs.
8. Preserve year-only dates as year-only dates.
9. Import coordinates already contained in the file.
10. Leave places without coordinates unresolved without external geocoding.
11. Search for a person.
12. Set that person as root.
13. Display multiple ancestor generations.
14. Correctly show missing ancestor slots.
15. Correctly handle pedigree collapse.
16. Display expected vs known ancestor slots per generation.
17. Display unique-person counts separately.
18. Show completeness of birth/death dates and places.
19. Select a person and inspect their information.
20. Display geographically known ancestor locations.
21. Export the tree or fan chart as SVG.
22. Export a configurable vector-oriented PDF.
23. Export a user-selected raster resolution as PNG.
24. Generate large-format PDF output without converting the complete visualization to a low-resolution screenshot.
25. Replace or clear the dataset.
26. Refresh the hosted application without genealogy data having been stored on an application server.
27. Run automated tests successfully.
28. Automatically deploy successful `main` builds to GitHub Pages.

